==Aftermath and legacy==
[[File:Steve hodge with maradona shirt.jpg|thumb|150px|Steve Hodge posing with the jersey worn by Maradona in the match, which he swapped with him]]
Hodge swapped shirts with Maradona after the game; Hodge lent out the Argentine's jersey to the [[National Football Museum]] in the 2000s. The game added hugely to the rivalry between the two teams.<ref name="goal" /> In Argentina, the game was seen as revenge for the Falklands War and for what they still see as [[Argentina–England football rivalry#1966 World Cup|the unfair game]] in the [[1966 FIFA World Cup|1966 World Cup]]. The former Argentine international [[Roberto Perfumo]] stated that "In 1986, winning that game against England was enough. Winning the World Cup was secondary for us. Beating England was our real aim."<ref name="OSM" />

The majesty of Maradona's second goal, the solo run from midfield, and the notoriety of his first, the "hand of God", saw the French newspaper ''[[L'Équipe]]'' describe him as "half-angel, half-devil".<ref name="Diego"/> Although the first goal proved highly controversial in England, Maradona's second goal was nevertheless recognized all over the world for its brilliance. A notable example of the English appreciation of his genius occurred in a 2002 poll conducted by [[Channel 4]], where the UK public voted Maradona's performance sixth in the list of the [[100 Greatest (TV series)#Episodes|100 Greatest Sporting Moments]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.channel4.com/entertainment/tv/microsites/G/greatest_sporting/results.html|title=100 Greatest Sporting Moments – Results|year=2002|publisher=[[Channel 4]]|location=London|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020204090913/http://www.channel4.com/entertainment/tv/microsites/G/greatest_sporting/results.html |archive-date=4 February 2002|url-status=dead|access-date=13 August 2019}}</ref> Outside the Estadio Azteca a statue of Maradona scoring the goal was erected to commemorate the moment.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://worldrec.info/2007/04/28/messis-goal-better-than-maradonas-goal-of-the-century/ |title=Messi's goal better than Maradona's goal of the century? |date=28 April 2007 |access-date=10 April 2012 |archive-date=12 October 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071012102712/http://worldrec.info/2007/04/28/messis-goal-better-than-maradonas-goal-of-the-century/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Argentina went on to win the [[1986 FIFA World Cup]] by defeating [[Germany national football team|West Germany]] in the [[1986 FIFA World Cup Final|final match]], as well as finishing runners-up in the [[1990 FIFA World Cup]]. England's Lineker won the Golden Boot for being top scorer in the 1986 FIFA World Cup. With a similar squad, England finished fourth in the 1990 FIFA World Cup, their highest finish since 1966.

The two teams have since met three times in World Cup matches. Argentina won a round-of-16 match in the shoot-out at the [[1998 FIFA World Cup]], after one [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty kick]] was awarded to each side, [[David Beckham]] was sent off, [[Sol Campbell]] had a goal disallowed and [[Michael Owen]] scored his famous goal. At the [[2002 FIFA World Cup]], the teams met in the group stage, and the match, which began at 12:30 PM UK time, was described as the "longest lunch break in history" as millions in England stopped their jobs and activities to watch the game on TV. England won 1–0 courtesy of a foul by [[Mauricio Pochettino]] on Michael Owen. Beckham's penalty kick won the match for England and Argentina later failed to advance to the knockout round.<ref name=top10/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=O_EyAAAAIBAJ&pg=5479,1886237&dq=longest+lunch+break+england+argentina&hl=en|title=The Free Lance-Star – Google News Archive Search|website=news.google.com}}</ref> In [[2026 FIFA World Cup|2026]], the teams met in the semifinals. England got off to an early lead after a goal scored by [[Anthony Gordon (footballer)|Anthony Gordon]] before goals by [[Enzo Fernández]] and [[Lautaro Martínez]] gave Argentina a 2–1 win.

On 17 August 2015, Maradona visited [[Ali Bin Nasser]], the referee of the 1986 World Cup quarter-final, at his home in Tunisia, and paid tribute to him by giving him an Argentine jersey bearing his signature, and referred to him as "my eternal friend".<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.marca.com/en/2015/08/17/en/football/international_football/1439845054.html |title=International football: Maradona holds up his hands in apology |work=Marca |date=17 August 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://elpais.com/elpais/2015/08/18/inenglish/1439899213_516413.html|title=Maradona visits Tunisian referee who awarded him 1986 "Hand of God" goal|first=Alejandro|last=Rebossio|date=18 August 2015}}</ref>

In April 2022, it was announced that the shirt worn by Maradona in that match would be auctioned by multinational company [[Sotheby's]]. The shirt was property of England's Steve Hodge, who exchanged shirts with Maradona at the end of the match. Hodge had kept the piece for over 16 years until he lent it to the [[National Football Museum]] in [[Manchester]] to be exhibited there.<ref>[https://tn.com.ar/deportes/futbol/2022/04/08/el-paso-a-paso-y-los-requisitos-para-poder-comprar-la-mitica-camiseta-de-diego-maradona/ El paso a paso y los requisitos para poder comprar la mítica camiseta de Diego Maradona] on TN, 8 April 2022</ref> The shirt was estimated to sell for more than [[Pound sterling|£]]4 million (US[[US Dollar|$]]5.25 million).<ref name=hogshirt>{{cite web |last=Grez |first=Matías |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2022/04/06/football/diego-maradona-hand-of-god-shirt-auction-spt-intl/index.html |title=Diego Maradona's 'Hand of God' shirt estimated to sell for more than $5 million at auction |work=CNN |date=6 April 2022 |access-date=6 April 2022}}</ref>
{{cquote|I have been the proud owner of this item for over 35 years, since Diego and I swapped shirts in the tunnel after the famed match... It was an absolute privilege to have played against one of the greatest and most magnificent football players of all time.<ref name=hogshirt/>
| source = Steve Hodge about the 'Hand of God' shirt
}}
On 4 May 2022, the shirt was sold for £7,142,500 (US$9.2 million), according to Sothebys' sources.<ref>[https://www.infobae.com/america/deportes/2022/05/04/record-historico-subastaron-la-mitica-camsieta-que-maradona-uso-ante-inglaterra-por-casi-usd-9-millones/ Récord histórico: subastaron la mítica camiseta que Maradona usó ante Inglaterra por más de USD 9 millones] on Infobae, 4 May 2022</ref> The price fetched a new record for an item of sports memorabilia.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2022/may/04/diego-maradona-hand-of-god-shirt-sold-for-record-71m-at-auction|title=Diego Maradona ‘hand of God’ shirt sold for record £7.1m at auction|date=4 May 2022|website=The Guardian|author-first1=Nadia|author-last1=Khomami}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.npr.org/2022/05/04/1096725190/maradona-hand-of-god-world-cup-shirt-auction|title=Maradona's 'Hand of God' shirt fetches $9.3M at auction, a sports memorabilia record|website=NPR|date=4 May 2022}}</ref>

In July 2026, the [[Argentina national rugby union team|Argentina national rugby team]] played their [[2026 Nations Championship Southern Hemisphere Series|2026 Nations Championship]] match vs [[England national rugby union team|England]] wearing a kit that was an exact replica of the model worn in 1986, including details like the silver numbers on back. The kit –manufactured by ''Pumas'' kit supplier Le Coq Sportif, the same than Argentina in 1986– was a limited edition to commemorate the 40th. anniversary of the historic football match.<ref name=espn1>[https://www.espn.com.ar/rugby/nota/_/id/17017869/rugby-los-pumas-inglaterra-camiseta-argentina-nations-championship-diego-maradona La camiseta homenaje de Los Pumas al histórico Argentina vs. Inglaterra del Mundial 1986] on espn.com.ar. 18 Jul 2026</ref><ref>[https://www.tycsports.com/rugby/los-pumas-sorprendieron-con-una-camiseta-inspirada-en-mexico-86-para-enfrentar-a-inglaterra-id750579.html Los Pumas usaron una camiseta inspirada en México ’86 en la derrota ante Inglaterra] on tycsports.com. 18 Jul 2026</ref>

